A||N||6||I'mSmall||Ignition timing||||5||
Z||000000||I'mSmall||10-31-2001||09:47 AM||||I need to know what the V.E.C.I. label for a 2.8 V6. I have an 86 se with a v6 but the vin and V.E.C.I. label are for the 4 cylinder. <P> SOMEONE PLEASE HELP ME !!!!!!  thank you.||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-31-2001||12:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by I'mSmall:<BR><B>I need to know what the V.E.C.I. label for a 2.8 V6. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>You need to know what the label <B>says</B>, you mean?  For setting the ignition timing on a stock V6 Fiero?  If that's what you're asking, the answer is this:  Ground the diagnostic terminal, and set the ignition timing so that the average reading with the timing light hooked to cylinder #1, and with the timing light hooked to cylinder #4, is 10 degrees before top dead center.      <P>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Leadfoot||10-31-2001||05:48 PM||||Someone here mentioned that you can connect the timing light to the coil lead.  This will allow you to see 2 strobed marks.  One is the #1 cylinder and the other the #4.  Just move the distributor so that both markers are at equal distances from 10 deg.||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||10-31-2001||06:14 PM||||I recently timed my car with a light, and now im confused.  When you guys are mentioning 10 degrees from TDC what are you meaning?  I guess what I mean is I don't know where TDC is represented on the little notches next to the flywheel.  Someone want to take the time to explain it in laymans terms really quick.  I'd appreciate it!<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jeff Blowers||10-31-2001||08:30 PM||||This is really great information as I have had the same question.  It seems like there is still a couple of unknowns here though - grounding the diagnostic terminals?  I think that is the first couple of pins on the ECM connector (A & B ??)  Also, beer_stud_21<BR>asked a good question regarding the degree markings.  Seems to me that the plate has 2 degree markings, but I thought the span was only 8 degrees?||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||11-01-2001||12:49 AM||||Folks, 10 degrees BTDC (before top dead center) is the 1st valley after the 1st peak going clockwise as you look at the harmonic balancer from the right side of the car. The 1st peak is 12 degrees and each peak OR valley is 2 degrees.  Hopefully, your cars still have the timing indicator still mounted on the front cover.  The diagnostic connector is covered on this webpage much better than I can cover it here (well actually I'm being lazy).  <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/fiero.htm</A>  Hope this helps. GL||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||11-01-2001||08:29 AM||||I guess this is a good time to mention that there are usually 3 marks on the crank pulley (harmonic damper).  The one you want to use, is the widest one.<P>The first time I have the right rear (RR) inner fender off the car, I clean the area and lightly sand the damper in the area of the (wide) timing mark.  Then I apply a swatch of screaming orange paint on either side of that wide slot.  Result:  A black slot in the middle of a swatch of orange.  It sticks out like an injured appendage <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">. There's no question you're looking at the right mark  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>Hope you people aren't tired of hearing about my little idea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">, but it's one of my favorite tips.          ||||reg||1
